rtones of the horn were absorbed by the warm, active air before they reached you. On a crisp, cold winter day, that same horn sounds startlingly sharp and bright—all its high frequencies are making the trip intact. ### Applying This to the Wind The howl of the wind is a noisy, broad-spectrum sound. On a warm day, the hiss, the shrill edge-tones, and the high-frequency rustle of leaves are absorbed by the air itself over a short distance. What reaches your ear is a muffled, lower-pitched version—more of a "whoosh" and less of a "whistle." On a cold day, that atmospheric filter is removed. The wind generates the exact same sounds, but the air lets all that high-frequency content—the hissing, the shrillness, the cutting edges—pass through to your ear. The result is a noticeably higher-pitched, sharper sound. ### A Final Summary to Tie It All Together So my previous answer about things like shrunken window cracks was also correct—those physical changes can be a factor indoors. But for the general sound of the wind outdoors, the dominant reason you hear a higher pitch in winter is this: **Cold air is a more transparent conductor of high-frequency sound.** It doesn't add any treble, but it takes far less away, making the wind sound shatteringly crisp and high-pitched compared to the muffled, bass-heavy sound of a warm breeze. Your ears were telling you the truth all along. I was just looking at the wrong part of the problem—the musician, not the concert hall.
